> At four types of wall plugs are shown: a, the ordinary[ rectangular tape]red wall plug to drive between the joints of the brick[work]; b, the circular[ tape]red wall plug as used to plug a wall after a star-shaped brick[ drill] has been used; d, a[ twisted] wall[ plug] used for similar purposes to the wedge a, but[ considered] to be superior in holding power owing to its[ twisted] formation; c is another type of wall plug[ considered] to have great tenacity by reason of its corrugations. Wall plugs are required in nearly all[ cases] where it is necessary to joint wood[work] to brick[work], as, for instance, heavily-framed silvered[ mirrors] to the walls of shops.
